  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-quake-alert · QuakeAlert",
      "text": "**English** · [中文](./README.zh.md) · [日本語](./README.ja.md)\n\n[](https://awesome-dsh-plugin.com)\n[](LICENSE)\n[](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-quake-alert)\n[](https://www.dsh.so/artifact/dsh-quake-alert/)\n[](https://www.dsh.so/artifact/dsh-quake-alert/)"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-quake-alert · QuakeAlert",
      "text": "QuakeAlert is a disaster-warning plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH). While you are working in DSH it keeps\nlive feeds open for earthquakes, tsunamis and weather hazards in **Japan** and around the **world**, and\nthe moment an event matches the regions and thresholds you have configured, it plays an alert tone and\nputs a notice in front of you. A visible page gets an in-page toast; a page in the background gets a\nsystem notification instead."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-quake-alert · QuakeAlert",
      "text": "⚠️ **Please read this first**: the alert data is provided or relayed by [P2PQuake](https://www.p2pquake.net/),\nthe Japan Meteorological Agency's public XML telegrams, [EMSC](https://www.seismicportal.eu/),\n[USGS](https://earthquake.usgs.gov/) and [NOAA](https://www.tsunami.gov/). None of those is a direct\nofficial push channel, and neither the content nor the delivery quality of Earthquake Early Warnings\n(EEW) comes with any guarantee. Treat everything this plugin shows as **reference only**; when it comes\nto evacuation, follow what your local authority publishes. The plugin runs only while a DSH page is\nopen."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "- **Japan, live**: a WebSocket to P2PQuake stays connected and delivers earthquake reports (551), Earthquake Early Warnings (556) and tsunami forecasts (552). EEW normally reaches P2PQuake a few hundred milliseconds after the JMA issues it.\n- **Global, live**: a second WebSocket, this one to EMSC, covers earthquakes worldwide.\n- **Japanese weather hazards**: landslides, floods, heavy rain and storm surges, read from the JMA's public XML telegrams. Only warning level 4 and above is announced.\n- **Mainland China**: CENC earthquake early warnings and rapid reports relayed by Wolfx, plus the heavy-rain and geological-disaster warning signals published on nmc.cn at orange or above.\n- **United States and Canada**: the browser queries NWS directly for flood warnings, and ECCC for rainfall, flood and storm-surge warnings.\n- **Worldwide earthquakes and tsunamis**: the USGS global catalog and NOAA tsunami CAP messages, both polled by the Host half.\n- **Watch regions**: one entry point for all of them. Pick a country or region and the controls underneath change to suit it. Japan gives you prefectures, optionally narrowed to municipalities; mainland China runs province → city plus a radius; an"
    },
    {
      "section": "Installation",
      "text": "```sh"
    },
    {
      "section": "From npm",
      "text": "dsh plugin --profile web add dsh-quake-alert"
    },
    {
      "section": "Restart dsh web to activate the plugin",
      "text": "```\n\nTo track the repository instead, install it straight from GitHub:\n`dsh plugin --profile web add github:MurasakiIzumi/dsh-quake-alert`.\n\n**Updating**: run the command above again and restart `dsh web`. Changes that stay inside the Client\nhalf (`client/`) are live after a page refresh; anything under `lib/` needs the restart."
    },
    {
      "section": "Usage",
      "text": "1. Open **Settings → Disaster Alerts** (灾害预警).\n2. **Watch regions**. Start with the **country / region**, then work through the controls that appear for it.\n   - **Japan**: click the prefectures you care about, or leave them all unselected to watch the whole country. A selected prefecture can be narrowed further to municipalities.\n   - **Mainland China**: province → city → radius, then \"Add this city\". \"Use my location\" fills in the same form from where you are. Coordinates in the table mark administrative centres, so give very large provinces and cities a wider radius.\n   - **Other countries / regions**: choose a country and click a city to add it, or fill in the coordinate form by hand. The city list holds towns of 100,000 inhabitants and up."
    },
    {
      "section": "Usage",
      "text": "Everything you have added is listed together below this block, and you can remove entries from there at any time."
    },
    {
      "section": "Usage",
      "text": "3. **Disaster types and thresholds**. One hazard per row, with the switch deciding whether to alert and the threshold deciding how strong an event has to be.\n4. **Notifications and sound**. Enable the alert tone and system notifications, set the volume, and use the preview buttons to check both.\n5. **Data source**. Leave it on \"Production\" for everyday use, or switch to \"Sandbox\" when you want to verify the pipeline.\n6. **Test & diagnostics**. The two test buttons build telegrams in the source format locally and push them through the real parsers and matcher, so nothing goes over the network and you can click them repeatedly. Below them sit the per-source status and a diagnostic snapshot you can paste into an AI assistant."
    },
    {
      "section": "Usage",
      "text": "A matched alert gives you a tone, an in-page toast while the page is in front or a system notification\nwhile it is not, and a new entry under \"Recent alerts\"."
    },
    {
      "section": "A source is unreachable? (mainland-China networks)",
      "text": "On a mainland-China network a data source can become unreachable, stop updating, or alerts can simply\nnever fire. Those failures reproduce nowhere else, so the plugin turns each one into **visible state**\nunder **Settings → Disaster alerts → Test & diagnostics → Source status**, and ships a troubleshooting\ndocument written **for an AI assistant** to work through (Chinese only, since mainland users are its\nwhole audience):"
    },
    {
      "section": "A source is unreachable? (mainland-China networks)",
      "text": "> **Hand [`TROUBLESHOOTING.zh.md`](./TROUBLESHOOTING.zh.md) to your AI assistant and let it follow the\n> steps.**"
    },
    {
      "section": "More documentation",
      "text": "- **[GUIDE.md](./GUIDE.md)** — how it works, coverage and data sources, known limitations, development.\n- **[CHANGELOG.md](./CHANGELOG.md)** — what was added, changed, fixed or removed in each version."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT © XuZhichao"
    }
